{"schema_version":1,"snapshot":"2021-12-03","source":{"repository":"matthiaskoenig/pkdb_analysis","path":"tests/data/testdata_concise_false.zip","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/matthiaskoenig/pkdb_analysis/develop/tests/data/testdata_concise_false.zip"},"sid":"PKDB00485","namespace":"pkdb","name":"Scott1988","licence":"closed","access":"public","date":"2021-07-08","creator":"janekg","curators":["janekg","mkoenig"],"substances":["caffeine (137X)"],"identifiers":{"source_study_sid":"PKDB00485","pmid":"3356110","pmcid":null,"doi":null,"pkdb_sid":"PKDB00485"},"reference":{"title":"Caffeine clearance and biotransformation in patients with chronic liver disease.","date":"1988-05-24","journal":"Clinical science (London, England : 1979)","authors":["N R Scott","D Stambuk","J Chakraborty","V Marks","M Y Morgan"],"abstract":"1. The clearance and biotransformation of caffeine (1,3,7-trimethylxanthine) were investigated in eight healthy control subjects and 16 patients with cirrhosis, by measuring serial serum caffeine concentrations and recoveries of methylxanthine metabolites in urine for 48 h after a 400 mg oral caffeine load. 2. In the control group, the mean (+/- SD) serum caffeine clearance was 1.3 +/- 0.4 ml min-1 kg-1 and a mean of 56.4 +/- 16.5% of the administered caffeine was recovered from the urine over 48 h as methyluric acids and methylxanthines. The majority of the metabolites were excreted in the first 24 h period and only 2.0 +/- 1.4% of the administered caffeine was excreted unchanged. 3. Patients with compensated cirrhosis (n = 10) metabolized caffeine similarly to the control subjects. Thus the mean serum caffeine clearance was 1.4 +/- 1.2 ml min-1 kg-1 and a mean of 57.2 +/- 11.7% of the administered caffeine was recovered from the urine over 48 h. The majority of the metabolites were excreted in the first 24 h; the pattern of metabolic excretion was unaltered and only 2.2 +/- 0.9% of the administered caffeine was excreted unchanged. 4. In the patients with decompensated cirrhosis (n = 6), significant changes were observed in caffeine metabolism. The mean serum caffeine clearance (0.4 +/- 0.2 ml min-1 kg-1) was significantly impaired compared with controls (P less than 0.01) and a significant delay was observed in metabolite excretion in the urine.(ABSTRACT TRUNCATED AT 250 WORDS)"},"row_counts":{"groups":47,"individuals":0,"interventions":1,"outputs":12,"timecourses":0,"scatters":0},"entity_counts":{"groups":5,"individuals":0,"interventions":1,"outputs":12,"timecourses":0,"scatters":0},"groups":[{"study_name":"Scott1988","measurement_type":"healthy","group_count":"24","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":16,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":807,"characteristica_pk":17904,"mean":null,"choice":"N","value":null},{"study_name":"Scott1988","measurement_type":"species","group_count":"24","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":24,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":807,"characteristica_pk":17905,"mean":null,"choice":"homo sapiens","value":null},{"study_name":"Scott1988","measurement_type":"overnight fast","group_count":"24","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":24,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":807,"characteristica_pk":17906,"mean":null,"choice":"Y","value":null},{"study_name":"Scott1988","measurement_type":"sex","group_count":"24","group_name":"all","max":null,"substance":null,"count":10,"calculation_type":"sample mean","group_parent_pk":null,"sd":null,"unit":null,"se":null,"min":null,"cv":null,"median":null,"group_pk":807,"characteristica_pk":17907,"mean":null,"choice":"M","value":null},{"study_name":"Scott1988","measurement_type":"sex","group_count":"24","group_name